<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikhwan revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ikhwan rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ikhwan revolt was an uprising of the Ikhwan, the Wahhabi tribal militia that had helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, against his authority. Led by Faisal al-Dawish and others, they objected to modern innovations and his ban on raids into British-protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>. Ibn Saud defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabilla">Battle of Sabilla</a> in 1929, and the last rebels surrendered in 1930.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikhwan-revolt.jpg" alt="Ikhwan revolt" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: سناح عبد العزيز بن سعد · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_ikhwan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Ikhwan, a Wahhabi Bedouin tribal army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Bajad and Faisal al-Duwaish, were the main instrument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a>'s campaigns of unification, and by the end of 1925 they had helped conquer most of the territory that later became Saudi Arabia. On 10 January 1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> declared himself King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, and on 27 January 1927 he took the title of King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> turned from conquest to diplomacy, border control and administration, the Ikhwan, who were also a religious movement, came into conflict with him. They opposed his negotiations with the British-backed governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, regarded fixed borders as incompatible with their understanding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> as innovations. Historians interpret the revolt both as a religious dispute and as a struggle over political authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A main cause of the rising was an Iraqi police fort built at Busaiya, on grazing lands of the Mutayr tribe. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> failed to stop it by diplomacy, Mutayr fighters raided Busaiya in late 1927, and raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> carried off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and sheep. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> bombed the tribes in Saudi-controlled territory from January to late February 1928. A general assembly of about 800 tribal leaders, ulema and Ikhwan figures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in November 1928 failed to end the raids, and in January 1929 one into Kuwait killed an American missionary, Dr. Bilkert.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabilla">Battle of Sabilla</a> in March 1929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>'s forces, armed with machine guns and supported by motor transport and modern communications, routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-mounted Ikhwan in about an hour; some 500 rebels and around 200 of the king's men were reported killed. Fighting in the Jabal Shammar region in August 1929 left some 1,000 dead, and an Ikhwan attack on the Awazim tribe on 5 October 1929 about 250. Al-Duwaish fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> in October and was handed over to Ibn Saud by the British, and on 10 January 1930 the remaining leaders surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Much of the Ikhwan leadership was killed or imprisoned. Al-Duwaish died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> on 3 October 1931, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Bajad died in 1932 after his capture. Surviving fighters were taken into regular Saudi units, and raiding across the frontiers declined. With the main internal challenge removed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> as the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia in September 1932. Some see in the revolt a seed of the 1979 Grand Mosque seizure: the father of its leader, Juhayman al-Otaybi, had fought against Ibn Saud at Sabilla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ikhwan revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
